<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The shared-mime-info packet for .satl source.

  Note on style: the rest of this tree writes an em dash as a double hyphen.
  This file cannot. XML forbids "- -" (closed up) anywhere inside a comment,
  and the failure is quiet in the worst way: update-mime-database prints a
  parse error, skips the packet, and still exits 0, so an install that ran
  without any visible failure leaves .satl files completely unregistered.
  Single hyphens throughout, deliberately.

  The file name is the mime type with '/' replaced by '-', which is not a
  convention this file is free to break: update-mime-database indexes the
  packet under whatever name it is installed as, and the icon lookup below
  derives from the type by the same substitution. Naming the packet
  satellite.xml would still work, but then three strings that must agree
  (packet, type, icon) would agree by accident rather than by construction.

  application/x-satellite, not text/x-satellite, for the reason the spec gives:
  the x- prefix marks a type not registered with IANA, and application/ is the
  correct top level for a file that is executed rather than read. A subclass of
  text/plain is declared anyway, so that an editor advertising support for
  text/plain still offers to open a .satl file, and so that a text editor is
  among the applications a file manager suggests. It buys compatibility, not an
  icon: see the note on generic-icon below for why this type deliberately has
  no icon fallback at all.
-->
<mime-info xmlns="http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/shared-mime-info">
  <mime-type type="application/x-satellite">
    <comment>Satellite source code</comment>
    <sub-class-of type="text/plain"/>

    <!--
      The icon is named after the type, because that is the name a file manager
      constructs on its own: it replaces '/' with '-' and searches the theme
      for it. Stating it explicitly costs nothing and makes the dependency on
      hicolor/*/mimetypes/application-x-satellite.png visible in this file
      rather than only in the Makefile that installs it.

      generic-icon names our OWN icon, which looks redundant and is the whole
      point. It is a fallback that deliberately refuses to fall back.

      GIO builds a GThemedIcon whose name list is [icon, generic, and the
      -symbolic form of each], and where generic-icon is absent it synthesises
      one from the media type: application/ yields application-x-generic. GTK
      then resolves that list with THEMES as the outer loop and names as the
      inner one, asking the active theme for every name before moving to the
      next theme. Adwaita defines no application-x-satellite, but it ships both
      text-x-script and application-x-generic, so whichever of those lands in
      the list answers first and hicolor, where our icon actually lives, is
      never reached.

      That is a lookup this file cannot win by choosing a BETTER fallback,
      because every plausible fallback is a name Adwaita already has. It can
      only win by naming no such icon at all. Pointing generic-icon back at
      application-x-satellite leaves a list of two names and their symbolic
      forms, none of which any stock theme defines, so the search falls through
      to hicolor and finds ours.

      Measured, not reasoned: with text-x-script here the lookup returned
      Adwaita/scalable/mimetypes/text-x-script.svg; with the element removed
      entirely it returned Adwaita's application-x-generic.svg, because the
      synthesised name is no better than the declared one.

      The cost is that a system without our artwork gets no icon rather than a
      generic page. That is the correct trade: a fallback is not worth having
      if it wins when the real icon is present.
    -->
    <icon name="application-x-satellite"/>
    <generic-icon name="application-x-satellite"/>

    <glob pattern="*.satl"/>

    <!--
      Content sniffing, so that a .satl file renamed or served without its
      extension is still recognised. weight is below the glob's default 50:
      the extension is the stronger signal, and this only has to win against
      text/plain, which registers no magic at all.

      The string is the include line every program begins with (see the loader
      in DESIGN 16). offset is a range rather than 0 because a file may open
      with comments or blank lines.

      The range is 4096 and not the 256 that looks sufficient. Measured against
      the seven programs in example/, the include line sits at byte 0, 0, 1, 1,
      394, 634 and 741: a licence or explanatory header pushes it a long way
      in, and bootstrap/lexer.satl alone rules out every bound below 741. 4096
      is chosen for headroom over that worst case rather than to fit it
      exactly, since the cost of a wider window is a slightly longer sniff of
      files that are not ours, and the cost of a narrow one is our own examples
      showing up as plain text.
    -->
    <magic priority="40">
      <match type="string" value="satellite.include(satellite)" offset="0:4096"/>
    </magic>
  </mime-type>
</mime-info>
